WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.179
This chapter covers bronchoscopy in two parts.

2
00:00:04.179 --> 00:00:14.415
First, the difference between a diagnostic and a therapeutic bronchoscopy, and the indications the RCP 203 slides list for each.

3
00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:31.050
Second, the medications used for the procedure: the three sedation drugs and their reversal agents, the premedications such as lidocaine and atropine, and the drugs used to control bleeding through the scope.

4
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:38.085
A diagnostic bronchoscopy answers the question, what's wrong?

5
00:00:38.085 --> 00:00:38.914
It looks.

6
00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:49.875
It is done to gather more information or to confirm a preliminary diagnosis, and biopsy and cytology samples are taken.

7
00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:56.046
A therapeutic bronchoscopy answers the question, how can we fix it?

8
00:00:56.046 --> 00:01:01.665
It treats airway problems rather than simply diagnosing them.

9
00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:13.178
The RCP 203 notes put it this way: diagnostics relate to finding a suspected disease, while therapeutics relate to treatment.

10
00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:17.507
The NBRC tip from RCP 105 sorts the procedures.

11
00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:24.784
Diagnostic means biopsy, bronchoalveolar lavage, washing and airway inspection.

12
00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:35.192
Therapeutic means removing mucus plugs and foreign bodies, controlling bleeding and relieving airway obstruction.

13
00:01:36.600 --> 00:02:09.770
The RCP 203 slides list the diagnostic indications: a lesion of unknown cause on the chest film, recurrent atelectasis or infiltrates, checking upper airway patency, finding the source of hemoptysis, an unexplained cough, a localized wheeze or stridor, suspicious sputum cytology, obtaining secretions, washings or biopsies, injury from toxic inhalation or aspiration, and problems with artificial airways.

14
00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:34.444
The therapeutic indications are helping with a difficult intubation, selective intubation of a mainstem bronchus, clearing mucus plugs that cause lobar or segmental atelectasis, removing abnormal tissue or foreign bodies, placing and assessing an airway stent, and balloon dilation for airway stenosis.

15
00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:42.614
The RCP 202 deck says therapeutic bronchoscopy is most commonly used for central airway obstruction.

16
00:02:42.614 --> 00:02:50.375
And if a question asks which procedure is both, fiberoptic bronchoscopy is an excellent answer.

17
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.627
Pause and sort these.

18
00:02:53.627 --> 00:02:58.412
One, a patient has lobar atelectasis from a mucus plug.

19
00:02:58.412 --> 00:03:03.892
Two, a patient has a lesion of unknown cause on the chest film.

20
00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:13.375
Which needs a diagnostic bronchoscopy, which needs a therapeutic one, and which single procedure can do both?

21
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:20.300
[6-second practice pause]

22
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:31.582
The mucus plug causing lobar atelectasis is a therapeutic indication, because the goal is to clear the plug and fix the problem.

23
00:03:31.582 --> 00:03:43.713
The lesion of unknown cause on the chest film is a diagnostic indication, because the goal is to find out what it is, with a biopsy or cytology.

24
00:03:43.713 --> 00:03:49.525
And the single procedure that can do both is fiberoptic bronchoscopy.

25
00:03:50.933 --> 00:04:02.744
The goal is a patient who is comfortable, coughs less, has fewer secretions and tolerates the scope, but is not completely asleep.

26
00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:16.554
Many hospitals now use midazolam, propofol or fentanyl instead of diazepam, and the RCP 202 deck says to focus on these three and their reversal agents.

27
00:04:16.554 --> 00:04:20.097
Midazolam, Versed, is a benzodiazepine.

28
00:04:20.097 --> 00:04:29.183
It gives sedation, anxiety relief and amnesia, but no pain relief, and it is reversed by flumazenil.

29
00:04:29.183 --> 00:04:39.086
Flumazenil can trigger seizures in susceptible patients, so it is not routinely given just to speed recovery.

30
00:04:39.086 --> 00:04:49.352
Fentanyl is an opioid that gives analgesia, and combined with midazolam the risk of respiratory depression rises.

31
00:04:49.352 --> 00:04:52.532
It is reversed by naloxone, Narcan.

32
00:04:52.532 --> 00:04:58.165
Propofol, Diprivan, is neither an opioid nor a benzodiazepine.

33
00:04:58.165 --> 00:05:09.158
It gives rapid sedation, can cause respiratory depression and hypotension, gives no analgesia, and has no reversal agent.

34
00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:15.281
Lidocaine is a local anesthetic that suppresses cough.

35
00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:26.542
RCP 105 gives five to ten milliliters of one to four percent by aerosol, or down the working channel starting at the vocal cords.

36
00:05:26.542 --> 00:05:38.415
Atropine dries secretions and reduces the vagal response and bronchospasm, given intramuscularly, though many centers have abandoned it.

37
00:05:38.415 --> 00:05:51.772
Morphine intramuscularly treats cough and pain, diazepam or another benzodiazepine intravenously gives sedation, and antibiotics are given if prescribed.

38
00:05:51.772 --> 00:06:00.415
The RCP 203 equipment list adds lidocaine, lidocaine with epinephrine, acetylcysteine and thrombin.

39
00:06:00.415 --> 00:06:15.867
Hess gives lidocaine differently, as two to three milliliter aliquots of one to two percent, up to five to seven milligrams per kilogram in total, but use the deck on this exam.

40
00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:26.673
For bleeding, the deck uses epinephrine, thrombin or iced saline, all given through the working channel.

41
00:06:26.673 --> 00:06:34.814
RCP 105 gives topical one to one thousand epinephrine, and says thrombin helps blood clot.

42
00:06:34.814 --> 00:06:44.311
Hess differs again: twenty milliliters of one to twenty thousand epinephrine if iced saline boluses fail.

43
00:06:44.311 --> 00:06:45.486
Use the deck.

44
00:06:45.486 --> 00:06:55.617
One gap remains: no source gives a dose or concentration for thrombin or acetylcysteine given through the scope.

45
00:06:57.033 --> 00:06:57.807
One more.

46
00:06:57.807 --> 00:07:05.286
After a bronchoscopy with midazolam and fentanyl, the patient's breathing is depressed.

47
00:07:05.286 --> 00:07:13.883
Which drug reverses each one, and which sedative used for bronchoscopy has no reversal agent at all?

48
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.800
[6-second practice pause]

49
00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:27.705
Flumazenil reverses midazolam, and naloxone, Narcan, reverses fentanyl.

50
00:07:27.705 --> 00:07:35.373
The combination of the two is exactly what raises the risk of respiratory depression.

51
00:07:35.373 --> 00:07:46.470
Remember that flumazenil can trigger seizures in susceptible patients, so it is not given routinely just to speed recovery.

52
00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:50.800
Propofol is the sedative with no reversal agent.

53
00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.764
Here is the chapter in brief.

54
00:07:54.764 --> 00:08:01.129
A diagnostic bronchoscopy looks: biopsy, lavage, washing and inspection.

55
00:08:01.129 --> 00:08:08.997
A therapeutic bronchoscopy treats: mucus plugs, foreign bodies, bleeding and obstruction.

56
00:08:08.997 --> 00:08:12.180
Fiberoptic bronchoscopy can do both.

57
00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:21.462
For sedation, midazolam is reversed by flumazenil and fentanyl by naloxone, and propofol has no reversal.

58
00:08:21.462 --> 00:08:34.900
Lidocaine suppresses cough, atropine dries secretions, and bleeding is controlled with epinephrine, thrombin or iced saline through the working channel.
